          %h2#Idea Idea
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functional+analysis"} functional analysis
            = succeed "," do
              %em distributional density
            %em distribution
            for short, is a generalization of the concept of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/density"} density
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integration"} integrated
            against a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bump+function"} bump function
            to produce a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/number"} number
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/density"} density
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/volume+form"} volume form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi dvol
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} dvol
            is fixed, then each other density is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
            relative to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi dvol
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} dvol
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+functions"} generalized functions
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit+of+a+sequence"} limits
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequences"} sequences
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+functions"} smooth functions
            (i.e. of
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-singular+distributions"} non-singular distributions
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delta+distributions"} delta distributions
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heaviside+distribution"} Heaviside distribution
            which behave like constant functions with an infinitely sharp spike or kink, respectively.
          %p
            Distributional densities appear notably as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+solutions"} fundamental solutions
            to linear
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+differential+equations"} partial differential equations
            (such as for the
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wave+equation"} wave equation
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klein-Gordon+equation"} Klein-Gordon equation
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propagators"} propagators
            of
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+fields"} free quantum fields
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+support"} singular support
            and their
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wave+front+set"} wave front set
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/PDE+theory"} PDE theory
            (for instance in the
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propagation+of+singularities+theorem"} propagation of singularities theorem
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/microlocal+analysis"} microlocal analysis
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operator-valued+distributions"} operator-valued distributions
          %p
            Often distributions are considered by default just on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+subsets"} open subsets
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean+space"} Euclidean space
            with its canonical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/volume+form"} volume form
            tacitly understood. But the concept of distributions makes sense more generally on general
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifolds"} smooth manifolds
            (at least). If these are equipped with the structure of a (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudo-Riemannian+manifold"}> pseudo-
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+manifold"} Riemannian manifold
            then the induced
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/volume+form"} volume form
            again identifies distributions with generalized functions.
          %p
            More in detail, given an actual
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/density"} density
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/volume+form"} volume form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi dvol
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} dvol
            on some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integration"} integration
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bump+functions"} bump functions
            (elements in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+vector+space"} topological vector space
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi C
                      %mi c
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty_c(X)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi dvol
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} dvol
            yields the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+linear+functional"} continuous linear functional
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msubsup
                            %mi C
                            %mi c
                            %mn ∞
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi X
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⟶
                        %mtd
                          %mi ℝ
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi b
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↦
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mo ∫
                            %mrow
                              %mi x
                              %mo ∈
                              %mi X
                          %mi b
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mi dvol
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  C^\infty_c(X)
                  \&amp;\longrightarrow&amp;
                  \\mathbb{R}
                  \\\
                  b &amp;\mapsto&amp; \int_{x \in X} b(x) dvol(x)
                  }
                  \\,.
          %p
            However, not every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+linear+functional"} continuous linear functional
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi C
                    %mi c
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty_c(X)
            arises this way. For example for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_0 \in X
            any point, the simple
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/evaluation"} evaluation
            map
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi δ
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 0
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi b
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\delta_{x_0}
                  \\;\colon\;
                  b
                  \\mapsto
                  b(x_0)
          %p
            is also a continuous linear functional on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi C
                    %mi c
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty_c(X)
            (the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delta+distribution"}> delta distribution
            ”). While this is not the integral against any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bump+function"} bump function
            times a fixed density, it is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit+of+a+sequence"} limit
            of integrations against any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequence"} sequence
            of bump functions (times the fixed density) whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/support"} support
            narrows in on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_0
            %em any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+linear+functional"} continuous linear functional
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi C
                      %mi c
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty_c(X)
          %p
            Various immediate variants of this definition may be considered. For instance if the space of “test functions”
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi C
                    %mi c
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty_c(X)
            is generalized to that of all
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+functions"} smooth functions
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compactly+supported+distributions"} compactly supported distributions
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schwartz+space"} Schwartz space
            of functions with all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derivatives"} derivatives
            rapidly decreasing, then one speaks of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tempered+distributions"} tempered distributions
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fourier+transform+of+distributions"} Fourier transform of distributions
          %p
            Most of the usual constructions of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+calculus"} differential calculus
            generalize from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+functions"} smooth functions
            to distributions, notably there is a concept of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derivative+of+distributions"} derivative of distributions
            (defined by generalizing the formula for
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integration+by+parts"} integration by parts
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+function"} partially defined
            on distributions, namely only when their singularity structure is compatible. In particular there is a concept of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback+of+distributions"} pullback of distributions
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product+of+distributions"} product of distributions
            compatible with that of smooth functions, but defined only whenever the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wave+front+sets"} wave front sets
            of the distributions involved satisfy suitable compatibility conditions. Taking this subtlety into account for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operator-valued+distributions"} operator-valued distributions
            appearing in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            is what leads to the concept of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wick+algebras"} Wick algebras
            (“normal ordering”), see there for more.
          %p
            Due to their potentially singular nature, there is more freedom in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extension+of+distributions"} extension of distributions
            than there is for smooth functions. Notably for extensions from the complement of a single point to that point the freedom is in choosing a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/point-supported+distribution"} point-supported distribution
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derivative+of+a+distribution"} derivatives
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delta-distributions"} delta-distributions
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time-ordered+products"} time-ordered products
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operator-valued+distributions"} operator-valued distributions
            it is precisely this freedom in choosing point-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extensions+of+distributions"} extensions of distributions
            which in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            is known as “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"}> renormalization
            ”.
          %h2#Definitions Definition
          %p We first recall the
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#TraditionalDefinition"}> Traditional definition
              \.
          %p
            Then we consider the axiomatic reformulation in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monads"} monads
            following
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Kock11"} Kock 11
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#CharacterizationByMonads"} Characterization by monads
          %h3#TraditionalDefinition As continuous linear functionals
          %p
            Distributions come in various flavors, depending on what spaces of functions they act on. The functions they act on are called
            = succeed ";" do
              %strong test functions
          %p The widest (and generally the default) notion is as follows.
          #CompactlySupportedTestFunctions.num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              %strong (compactly supported test functions)
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ⊂
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \subset \mathbb{R}^n
              a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
              given as an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+subset"} open subset
              of a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean+space"} Euclidean space
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+vector+space"} topological vector space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi C
                      %mi c
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty_c(X)
              of
              %em compactly supported test functions
              is the following
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  the underlying set is the set of
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bump+functions"} bump functions
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+functions"} smooth functions
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                        %mo →
                        %mi ℝ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to \mathbb{R}
                  to the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
                  with
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+support"} compact support
              %li
                %p
                  equipped with evident
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+vector+space"} real vector space
                  structure given by pointwise addition and pointwise multipication of functions.
              %li
                %p
                  equipped with the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topology
                  which is the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+topology"} metric topology
                  induced from the family of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/seminorms"} seminorms
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi ρ
                          %mrow
                            %mi K
                            %mo ,
                            %mi α
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %munder
                          %mi sup
                          %mrow
                            %mi x
                            %mo ∈
                            %mi K
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %msup
                          %mo ∂
                          %mi α
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho_{K, \alpha}(f) = \sup_{x \in K} |\partial^{\alpha} f|
                %p
                  where
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi K
                        %mo ⊆
                        %mi U
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K \subseteq U
                  is
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+topological+space"} compact
                  and
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi α
                        %mo =
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi α
                          %mn 1
                        %mo ,
                        %mi …
                        %mo ,
                        %msub
                          %mi α
                          %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha = (\alpha_1, \ldots, \alpha_n)
                  is a multi-index and
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mo ∂
                          %mi α
                        %mo =
                        %mfrac
                          %mrow
                            %msup
                              %mo ∂
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi α
                                  %mn 1
                          %mrow
                            %mo ∂
                            %msup
                              %mi x
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi α
                                  %mn 1
                        %mi …
                        %mfrac
                          %mrow
                            %msup
                              %mo ∂
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi α
                                  %mi n
                          %mrow
                            %mo ∂
                            %msup
                              %mi x
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi α
                                  %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial^{\alpha} = \frac{\partial^{\alpha_1}}{\partial x^{\alpha_1}} \ldots \frac{\partial^{\alpha_n}}{\partial x^{\alpha_n}}
                %p
                  is the corresponding
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+operator"} differential operator
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+vector+space"} topological vector space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi C
                      %mi c
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty_c(X)
              of compactly supported test functions (def.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CompactlySupportedTestFunctions"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+convex+topological+vector+space"} locally convex
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+space"} complete
              with respect to its
              = succeed ";" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/uniformity"} uniformity
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inductive+limit"} inductive limit
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fr%C3%A9chet+spaces"} Fréchet spaces
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi C
                      %mi c
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_c^{\infty}(K)
              (each of which has
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+set"} empty
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interior"} interior
              as a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subspace"} subspace
              of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi C
                        %mi c
                        %mn ∞
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_c^{\infty}(U)
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Baire+category+theorem"} Baire category theorem
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi C
                      %mi c
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_c^{\infty}(U)
              is not itself a
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fr%C3%A9chet+space"} Fréchet space
          #DistributionOnOpenSubsetOfEuclideanSpace.num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              %strong (distribution on Euclidean space)
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ⊂
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \subset \mathbb{R}^n
              be a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
              given as an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+subset"} open subset
              of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean+space"} Euclidean space
            %p
              A
              %strong distribution on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+linear+functional"} continuous linear functional
              of the form
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi C
                      %mi c
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty_c(X) \longrightarrow \mathbb{R}
            %p
              from the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+convex+space"} locally convex
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+vector+space"} topological vector space
              of compactly supported test functions (def.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CompactlySupportedTestFunctions"}
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
            %p
              The space of distributions on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is denoted
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒟
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}'(X)
              (see also remark
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#NotationForSpaceOfDistributions"}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi 𝒟
                            %mo ′
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi X
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo ×
                            %msubsup
                              %mi C
                              %mi c
                              %mn ∞
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi X
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⟶
                          %mtd
                            %mi ℝ
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi S
                            %mo ,
                            %mi ϕ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↦
                          %mtd
                            %mi S
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi ϕ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \\mathcal{D}'(X) \times C_c^{\infty}(X) &amp;\longrightarrow&amp; \mathbb{R}
                    \\\
                    (S, \phi) &amp;\mapsto&amp; S(\phi)
                    }
            %p
              given by
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/evaluation"} evaluation
            %p
              Often one writes
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mi S
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle S, \phi\rangle
              instead of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S(\phi)
              = succeed "-topology," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} *
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi 𝒟
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle -, \phi\rangle \;\colon\; \mathcal{D}'(U) \to \mathbb{R}
            %p
              continuous for all test functions
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi C
                      %mi c
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_c^\infty(U)
              is reflexive, this agrees with the weak topology.
          %p
            See at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+convex+topological+vector+space"} locally convex topological vector space
            the section
            %em
              %a{:href => "locally+convex+space#ContinuousLinearFunctionals"} Continuous linear functionals
            for alternative characterizations of the continuity of distributions according to def.
            = succeed "." do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#DistributionOnOpenSubsetOfEuclideanSpace"}
          %p
            Notice that other natural topologies exist, such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/uniform+convergence"} uniform convergence
            on compact subsets of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi C
                    %mi c
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_c^\infty(U)
            (in this case, this agrees with uniform convergence on bounded subsets which usually goes by the name of the
            %em strong
            topology).
          #NotationForSpaceOfDistributions.num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              %strong (notation)
            %p
              On general grounds the symbols
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D(X)
              or
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒟
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}(X)
              or similar would seem evident notation for the space of distributions on a smooth manifold
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Laurent+Schwartz"} Laurent Schwartz
              in his seminal work (
              %a{:href => "#Schwartz50"}> Schwartz 50
              ) used
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒟
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}(X)
              to denote the space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi C
                      %mi c
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty_c(X)
              of compactly supported continuous functions, and then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒟
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}'(X)
              for its linear continuous dual, hence for the space of distributions (see also
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "#Hoermander90"} Hör#mander 90, below def. 2.1.1
          .num_example
            %h6#example Example
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon X \to \mathbb{R}
              is
              = succeed "," do
                %span.newWikiWord
                  locally integrable
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/locally+integrable+function"} ?
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
              the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lebesgue+integral"} Lebesgue integral
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mi f
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mo ∫
                      %mi X
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi d
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle f, \phi\rangle = \int_X f(x)\phi(x) d x
            %p
              is defined; in this way a function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              locally integrable over
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              may be regarded as a distribution on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              (explaining both the sense in which distributions are “generalized functions” and a reason for the angle-bracket notation for the evaluation pairing). In particular, there is an obvious inclusion
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi C
                      %mi c
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi 𝒟
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_c^{\infty}(X) \hookrightarrow \mathcal{D}'(X)
            %p
              and this inclusion turns out to be
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dense+subset"} dense
          %p
            Other notions of spaces of distributions, each endowed with the weak
            = succeed "-topology," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} *
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compactly+supported+distributions"} compactly supported distributions
                on
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mn ∞
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{\infty}(U)
                (test functions without compact support).
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tempered+distributions"} tempered distributions
                (usually on
                = succeed ")." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                        %mo =
                        %msup
                          %mi ℝ
                          %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U = \mathbb{R}^n
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schwartz+space"} Schwartz space
                = succeed ":" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒮
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derivatives"} derivatives
                (of any order) decays rapidly (goes to zero more quickly than any negative power of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |x|
                as
                = succeed ")." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mo →
                        %mn ∞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |x| \to \infty
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ρ
                        %mrow
                          %mi K
                          %mo ,
                          %mi α
                          %mo ,
                          %mi β
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %munder
                        %mi sup
                        %mrow
                          %mi x
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi K
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %msup
                        %mi x
                        %mi α
                      %msup
                        %mo ∂
                        %mi β
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho_{K, \alpha, \beta}(\phi) = \sup_{x \in K} |x^\alpha \partial^\beta \phi|
              %p
                where
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi α
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi β
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta
                are multi-indices.
            %li
              %p
                Rapidly decaying distributions (usually on
                = succeed ")." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                        %mo =
                        %msup
                          %mi ℝ
                          %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U = \mathbb{R}^n
          #PullbackOfDistributionAlongSubmersion.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback+of+distributions"} pullback of distributions
                along
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/submersions"} submersions
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ⊂
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_1, X_2 \subset \mathbb{R}^n
              are two
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+subsets"} open subsets
              of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean+space"} Euclidean space
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 1
                    %mover
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    f \;\colon\; X_1 \overset{}{\longrightarrow} X_2
            %p
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/submersion"} submersion
              (i.e. its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential"} differential
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surjective+function"} surjective function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mi x
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mi x
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d f_x \;\colon\; T_x X_1 \to T_{f(x)} X_2
              for all
              = succeed ")," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in X_1
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+linear+functional"} continuous linear functional
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi 𝒟
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi 𝒟
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    f^\ast \;\colon\; \mathcal{D}'(X_2) \longrightarrow \mathcal{D}'(X_1)
            %p
              between spaces of distributions (def.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#DistributionOnOpenSubsetOfEuclideanSpace"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback+of+functions"} pullback of functions
              in that on a distribution represented by a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bump+function"} bump function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
              it is given by precomposition
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %mi b
                    %mo =
                    %mi b
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi f
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    f^\ast b = b \circ f
                    \\,.
            %p
              This is hence called the
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback+of+distributions"} pullback of distributions
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#Hoermander90"}> Hörmander 90, theorem 6.1.2
            )
          #DistributionsOnSmoothManifolds.num_defn
            %h6#definition_4 Definition
            %p
              %strong (distributions on smooth manifolds)
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              be a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+class"} equivalence class
              of
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  a choice of smooth
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/atlas"} atlas
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                          %msup
                            %mi ℝ
                            %mi n
                          %munderover
                            %mo ⟶
                            %mo ≃
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi ψ
                                %mi i
                          %msub
                            %mi U
                            %mi i
                          %mo ⊂
                          %mi X
                          %msub
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                            %mrow
                              %mi i
                              %mo ∈
                              %mi I
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\mathbb{R}^n \underoverset{\simeq}{\psi_i}{\longrightarrow} U_i \subset X\}_{i \in I}
              %li
                %p
                  for each
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi I
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i \in I
                  a distribution
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mi i
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %msup
                          %mi C
                          %mn ∞
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msup
                          %mi ℝ
                          %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo →
                        %mi ℝ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_i \;\colon\; C^\infty(\mathbb{R}^n)\to \mathbb{R}
                  on the
                  = succeed "th" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chart"} chart
              %li
                %p
                  such that for all pairs
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi i
                        %mo ,
                        %mi j
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi I
                        %mo ×
                        %mi I
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (i,j) \in I \times I
                  these component distributions are related on intersections of charts by pullback of distributions (def.
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#PullbackOfDistributionAlongSubmersion"}
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mi j
                        %mo =
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msubsup
                          %mi ψ
                          %mi i
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                            %mn 1
                        %mo ∘
                        %msub
                          %mi ψ
                          %mi j
                        %msup
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo *
                        %msub
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mi i
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_j =  (\psi_i^{-1} \circ \psi_j)^\ast  \phi_i
                  \.
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#Hoermander90"}> Hörmander 90, def. 6.3.3
            )
          %h3#CharacterizationByMonads As smooth linear functionals
          %p
            Since
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+functions"} smooth functions
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifolds"} smooth manifolds
            are the subject of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+functions"} smooth functions
            are naturally themselves
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+smooth+spaces"} generalized smooth spaces
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
          %p
            In particular we may ask how distributions in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functional+analysis"} functional analytic
            sense relate to the
            %em smooth linear functions
            on smooth spaces of smooth functions. Indeed, with respect to the natural formulation of differential geometry via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functorial+geometry"} functorial geometry
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"}> topos theory
            ) in terms of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeological+spaces"} diffeological spaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+sets"} smooth sets
            etc. it turns out that distributional densities are equivalently the smooth linear functionals on smooth spaces of smooth functions.
          %p This is discussed at
          %ul
            %li
              %em>
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributions+are+the+smooth+linear+functionals"} distributions are the smooth linear functionals
              \.
          %h2#operations_on_distributions Operations on distributions
          %h3#inducing_operations_by_dual_extension Inducing operations by dual extension
          %p
            As
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒟
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}'(U)
            is dual to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi C
                      %mi c
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_c^\infty(U)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi C
                    %mi c
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_c^\infty(U)
            induces a corresponding linear operator on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒟
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}'(U)
            in the obvious way. Given
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %msubsup
                    %mi C
                    %mi c
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msubsup
                    %mi C
                    %mi c
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  F\colon C_c^\infty(U) \to C_c^\infty(U)
          %p we define
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi F
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi 𝒟
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝒟
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  F^*\colon \mathcal{D}'(U) \to \mathcal{D}'(U)
          %p according to the usual formula for dualities
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi F
                    %mo *
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  F^* S(\phi) = S(F \phi).
          %p
            However, since there is an obvious inclusion
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi C
                    %mi c
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝒟
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_c^\infty(U) \to \mathcal{D}'(U)
            induced by the standard inner product on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi C
                      %mi c
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_c^\infty(U)
            %strong extension
            operator. That is, instead of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi F
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F^*
            we want an operator
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi F
                    %mo †
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi 𝒟
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝒟
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F^\dagger \colon \mathcal{D}'(U) \to \mathcal{D}'(U)
            with the property that for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo ∈
                  %msubsup
                    %mi C
                    %mi c
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi \in C_c^\infty(U)
            then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi F
                    %mo †
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F^\dagger(\phi) = F(\phi)
            (identifying
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi C
                    %mi c
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_c^\infty(U)
            with its image in
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒟
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}'(U)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ι
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %msubsup
                    %mi C
                    %mi c
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝒟
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \iota \colon C_c^\infty(U) \to \mathcal{D}'(U)
            for the inclusion induced by the inner product. Then we want
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi F
                      %mo †
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ι
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi ι
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F^\dagger(\iota \phi) = \iota (F(\phi))
          %p If the extension exists, we have
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi F
                    %mo †
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ι
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi ι
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  F^\dagger(\iota \phi)(\psi) = \iota(F(\phi))(\psi) = \langle F(\phi), \psi \rangle
          %p
            Now suppose that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            has an adjoint, say
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi F
                      %mo +
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F^+
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi C
                    %mi c
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_c^\infty(U)
            is not a Hilbert space. Moreover, even if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            extends to the Hilbert completion the Hilbertian adjoint may not work since it may not define a continuous linear map on the subspace
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi C
                      %mi c
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_c^\infty(U)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi F
                    %mo +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F^+
            does exist then we have
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi F
                    %mo †
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ι
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi F
                    %mo +
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  F^\dagger(\iota \phi)(\psi) = \langle F(\phi), \psi \rangle = \langle \phi, F^+(\psi) \rangle
          %p
            In this case, the definition of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi F
                    %mo †
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F^\dagger
            on the whole of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒟
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}'(U)
            is obvious: simply take
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi F
                          %mo +
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {F^+}^*
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi F
                    %mo †
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi 𝒟
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝒟
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F^\dagger \colon \mathcal{D}'(U) \to \mathcal{D}'(U)
            is defined via the formula
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %msup
                    %mi F
                    %mo †
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi S
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi F
                    %mo +
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\langle F^\dagger(S),\phi\rangle = \langle S, F^+(\phi) \rangle
          %p
            If the ground field is
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}
            then this carries through essentially unchanged except for the fact that one does
            %strong not
            use the inner product on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi C
                    %mi c
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_c^\infty(U)
            but rather the associated bilinear pairing
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi U
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mi ψ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (\phi,\psi) = \int_U \phi \psi
          %p
            This is to ensure that the inclusion
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi C
                    %mi c
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝒟
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_c^\infty(U) \to \mathcal{D}'(U)
            is complex linear and not conjugate linear. Otherwise extending operators becomes complex.
          %p Two instances are of particular importance:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Multiplication by a smooth function
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi θ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi θ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta
                is any smooth function on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                (not necessarily compactly supported), then we can define
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi θ
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta \cdot S
                by observing that this multiplication is self-adjoint:
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                      %mi θ
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi ψ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi ψ
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mi θ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle \theta \cdot \phi, \psi \rangle = \langle \phi, \psi \cdot \theta\rangle
              %p
                where
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi ψ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi, \psi
                are arbitrary test functions. Thus we define
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi θ
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta \cdot S
                by
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                      %mi θ
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mi S
                      %mo ,
                      %mi ψ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                      %mi S
                      %mo ,
                      %mi θ
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mi ψ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle \theta \cdot S, \psi \rangle = \langle S, \theta \cdot \psi
            %li
              %p
                Differentiation. If
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mo ∂
                        %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial^i
                is partial differentiation with respect to the
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi i
                        %mi th
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i^{th}
                coordinate, then for test functions
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ψ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
                we have
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo ∫
                        %mi U
                      %msup
                        %mo ∂
                        %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ψ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi d
                      %mi x
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %msub
                        %mo ∫
                        %mi U
                      %mi ψ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %msup
                        %mo ∂
                        %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi d
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int_U \partial^i(\psi)(x) \phi(x)\; d x = -\int_U \psi(x) \partial^i(\phi)(x)\; d x
              %p
                by simple integration by parts and the fact that
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ϕ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ψ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi
                are compactly supported. Thus differentiation is
                = succeed "-adjoint" do
                  %em skew
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                      %msup
                        %mo ∂
                        %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi S
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                      %mi S
                      %mo ,
                      %msup
                        %mo ∂
                        %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle \partial^i(S), \phi\rangle = -\langle S, \partial^i(\phi) \rangle
              %p
                for all test functions
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ϕ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                      %msup
                        %mo ∂
                        %mi α
                      %mi S
                      %mo ,
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mi α
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                      %mi S
                      %mo ,
                      %msup
                        %mo ∂
                        %mi α
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle \partial^\alpha S, \phi\rangle = (-1)^{|\alpha|}\langle S, \partial^\alpha \phi \rangle
              %p
                where
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi α
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi α
                        %mn 1
                      %mo +
                      %mi …
                      %mo +
                      %msub
                        %mi α
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |\alpha| = \alpha_1 + \ldots + \alpha_n
                is the total degree of the multi-index.
          %p Thus derivatives of distributions are defined to all orders. Some examples are given in the section “examples”.
          %h3#MultiplicationsOfDistributions Multiplication of Distributions
          %p
            See at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multiplication+of+distributions"} multiplication of distributions
          %h2#examples Examples
          %p
            As explained above, any locally integrable function on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            defines a distribution on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
          %p
            For instance: if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_c(U)
            denotes the space of real-valued continuous functions with compact support in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            (topologized by uniform convergence on compacts), then a functional
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu: C_c(U) \to \mathbb{R}
            is essentially the same as a signed measure on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            (Riesz-Markov theorem), i.e., there is a unique signed measure
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mi m
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d m
            for which
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi U
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mi d
                  %mi m
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu(\phi) = \int_U \phi d m.
          %p
            Since the inclusion
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mo :
                  %msubsup
                    %mi C
                    %mi c
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ↪
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i: C_c^\infty(U) \hookrightarrow C_c(U)
            is continuous, it follows that a measure
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            defines a distribution by simple restriction along
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi C
                    %mi c
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi μ
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_c^\infty(U) \overset{i}{\to} C_c(U) \overset{\mu}{\to} \mathbb{R}
          %p
            Specializing further, consider any function of bounded variation on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo =
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U = \mathbb{R}
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi ℝ
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi d
                  %mi α
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int_{\mathbb{R}} f(x) d\alpha(x)
          %p
            is defined for all functions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            with compact support; this provides a measure
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mi α
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d\alpha
            and hence a distribution.
          %p
            A prototypical example of this is provided by the Heaviside function:
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H(x) = 1
            if
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo &gt;
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \gt 0
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \in C_c(\mathbb{R})
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi f
                  %mo ,
                  %mi d
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi ℝ
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi d
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle f, d H \rangle = \int_{\mathbb{R}} f(x) d H(x) = f(0)
          %p
            As a distribution, the Heaviside measure is the famous
            = succeed "." do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+distribution"} Dirac distribution
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi δ
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi d
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d H(x) = \delta_0(x) d x
          %p
            where of course the function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H(x)
            doesn’t have a derivative in the classical sense (i.e., as a function), but as a distribution, it does. Meanwhile,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H(x)
            is itself the derivative of a continuous function:
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi max
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G(x) = \max\{x, 0\}
          %p
            For an example of a distribution on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            which does not arise from a measure, consider the derivative of the Dirac distribution. (As a functional, it maps a test function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            to
            = succeed ".)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} -\phi'(0)
          %p
            These examples are by no means curiosities. A fairly deep theorem is that
            %em every
            distribution arises as a linear combination of derivatives of continuous functions:
          %p
            %strong Theorem:
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            be a distribution on an open domain
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo ⊆
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            of multi-indices
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
            and continuous functions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mi α
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_\alpha
            defined on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            for which
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi α
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi A
                  %msup
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi α
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mi α
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S = \sum_{\alpha \in A} \partial^\alpha g_\alpha
          %h2#applications Applications
          %p
            The theory of distributions (and more generally of
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/microlocal+analysis"} microlocal analysis
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            in its rigorous incarnation via
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"} causal perturbation theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+algebraic+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative algebraic quantum field theory
          %p
            For example the reason for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normal+ordered+products"} normal ordered products
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wick+algebras"} Wick algebras
            is given by the Hörmander criterion on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wave+front+sets"} wave front sets
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product+of+distributions"} product of distributions
            to be well defind, and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} renormalization
            is understood to be the freedom in choosing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extension+of+distributions"} extension of distributions
            of the resulting products of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+propagators"} Feynman propagators
          %p
            See also
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operator-valued+distribution"} operator-valued distribution
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wightman+axioms"} Wightman axioms
          %p
            A brief survey of applications of distribution theory to perturbative quantum field theory may be found
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://www1.jinr.ru/Archive/Pepan/v-31-7a/I_ktp_04_t.pdf"} here
          %p
            Within mathematics, distributions are quite commonplace; for example,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Georges+de+Rham"} Georges de Rham
            appropriated them for his theory of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/current+%28distribution+theory%29"} currents
          %ul
            %li
              %strong Theorem (Ehrenpreis, Malgrange):
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              be a linear differential operator on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
              with constant coefficients. Given a compactly supported smooth function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              on
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi u
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u
              to the equation
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                      %mi u
                      %mo =
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D u = f
          %p
            A proof is given in these
            %a{:href => "http://www-math.mit.edu/~helgason/hormander.pdf"} notes
            by Helgason. The basic idea is to prove there exists a
            %strong fundamental solution
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            such that
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                    %mi T
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi δ
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D T = \delta_0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi u
                  %mo =
                  %mi f
                  %mo *
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u = f * T
            is smooth. The existence of a fundamental solution involves a theorem of Paley-Wiener type.
          %h2#variants Variants
          %h3#in_synthetic_differential_geometry In synthetic differential geometry
          %p
            There is another point of view on distributions: that they
            %em can
            be modeled by actual functions provided that one admits infinite and infinitesimal quantities of the type used in Robinson
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonstandard+analysis"} nonstandard analysis
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+toposes"} smooth toposes
            that model the axioms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+geometry"} synthetic differential geometry
            and support a suitable notion of invertible
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+object"} infinitesimal objects
            and infinitely large integers.
          %p
            This is discussed in (
            %a{:href => "#MoerdijkReyes91"}> Moerdijk-Reyes 91
            ).
          %p
            which closely mirrors the original treatment in Robinson’s book Non-standard Analysis. Examples of models that support these axioms are the toposes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒵
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{Z}
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℬ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}
            described there.
          %h3#currents Currents
          %p
            In
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
            the distributions and generalized functions boil down to the same thing, so the terminology identifies them. But on a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
            = succeed ")" do
              %em densities
            %em Geometric asymptotics
            (
            %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/online_bks/surv14"}> free online
            ). While generalized functions pull back, distributions/generalized densities push forward (under some conditions, though).
          %p
            More generally one can study generalized differential
            = succeed "-forms" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mi α
                    %msup
                      %mi dx
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi α
                          %mn 1
                    %mo ∧
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mo ∧
                    %msup
                      %mi dx
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi α
                          %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sum f_\alpha dx^{\alpha_1}\wedge \cdot \wedge dx^{\alpha_k}
            = succeed "." do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/current+%28distribution+theory%29"} currents
            %em Principles of algebraic geometry
            by Griffiths and Harris) and in geometric measure theory (cf. the monograph by Federer).
          %h3#hyperfunctions_and_coulombeau_distributions Hyperfunctions and Coulombeau distributions
          %p
            Sometimes one considers larger spaces of distributions, where worse singularities than in Schwaryz theory are allowed. Most well known are the theory of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hyperfunctions"} hyperfunctions
            and the theory of
            = succeed "." do
              %strong Coulombeau distributions
          %h3#distributions_from_nonstandard_analysis Distributions from nonstandard analysis
          %p
            Distributions can be alternatively described using
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonstandard+analysis"} nonstandard analysis
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-singular+distribution"} non-singular distribution
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/order+of+a+distribution"} order of a distribution
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/support+of+a+distribution"} support of a distribution
              %ul
                %li
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/point-supported+distribution"} point-supported distribution
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scaling+degree+of+a+distribution"} scaling degree of a distribution
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product+of+a+distribution+with+a+smooth+function"} product of a distribution with a smooth function
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derivative+of+a+distribution"} derivative of a distribution
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+solution+of+a+partial+differential+equation"} generalized solution of a partial differential equation
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extension+of+distributions"} extension of distributions
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+distributions"} tensor product of distributions
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product+of+distributions"} product of distributions
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convolution+product+of+distributions"} convolution product of distributions
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/current+%28distribution+theory%29"} current (distribution theory)
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/H%C3%B6rmander+topology"} Hörmander topology
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fourier+transform+of+distributions"} Fourier transform of distributions
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere+distribution"} Lawvere distribution
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorification"} categorification
                of the concept of distributions)
          %h2#references References
          %p
            See also
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hyperfunction"} hyperfunction
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ultradistribution"} ultradistribution
            and references therein.
          %h3#traditional Traditional
          %p
            Generalized functions were introduced by S. L. Sobolev in 1935, and independently (under the name
            = succeed ")" do
              %em distributions
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Laurent+Schwartz"} Laurent Schwartz
            in the 1940’s, who unaware of Sobolev’s work developed an extensive theory for them. For an infinite-dimensional variant used in the foundation of Feynman
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"} path integral
            see also
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Connes+distribution"} Connes distribution
          %p The original articles include
          %ul
            %li#Schwartz50
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Laurent+Schwartz"}> Laurent Schwartz
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Théorie des distributions
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Israel+M.+Gelfand"}> Israel M. Gelfand
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Georgiy+E.+Shilov"} Georgiy E. Shilov
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Generalized functions
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://bookstore.ams.org/view?ProductCode=CHELGELFSET"} ISBN:978-1-4704-2885-3
            %li
              %p
                E. Magenes, G. Stampacchia,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Teoria delle distribuzioni
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 24
                %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-10967-6"} doi
            %li#Treves67
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fran%C3%A7ois+Tr%C3%A8ves"}> François Trèves
                ,
                %em Topological Vector Spaces, Distributions and Kernels
                (Academic Press, New York, 1967)
          %p Modern accounts:
          %ul
            %li#Hoermander90
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lars+H%C3%B6rmander"}> Lars Hörmander
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The analysis of linear partial differential operators
            %li
              %p
                Walter Rudin, chapter 6 of
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Functional analysis
            %li
              %p
                M. Grosser, E. Farkas, M. Kunzinger, R. Steinbauer,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em On the foundations of nonlinear generalized functions I, II
                %strong 153
                (2001)
            %li
              %p
                M. Kunzinger, R. Steinbauer,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Foundations of a nonlinear distributional geometry
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 71
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/current+%28distribution+theory%29"}> currents
            ):
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Victor+Guillemin"}> Victor Guillemin
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Shlomo+Sternberg"} Shlomo Sternberg
              = succeed "," do
                %em Geometric asymptotics
              = succeed "," do
                %strong 14
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://bookstore.ams.org/surv-14"} ams:surv-14
          %p Lecture notes:
          %ul
            %li
              Hasse Carlsson,
              %em Lecture notes on distributions
              (
              %a{:href => "http://www.math.chalmers.se/~hasse/distributioner_eng.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p and several chapters of the course
          %ul
            %li
              Erik P. van den Ban,
              %em Analysis on manifolds
              (2009) (
              %a{:href => "https://www.staff.science.uu.nl/~ban00101/anman2009/anman2009.html"} web
              \- especially lectures 1-3)
          %p
            Applications of distributions in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            are discussed in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                V. S. Vladimirov,
                = succeed "." do
                  %em Generalized functions in mathematical physics
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Equations of mathematical physics
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nikolay+Bogolyubov"}> Nikolay Bogolyubov
                , A. A. Logunov, I.T. Todorov,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Introduction to axiomatic quantum field theory
          %p
            Application of distributions in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            is discussed in
          %ul
            %li#Scharf95
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G%C3%BCnter+Scharf"} Günter Scharf
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Finite+Quantum+Electrodynamics+--+The+Causal+Approach"} Finite Quantum Electrodynamics – The Causal Approach
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.zentralblatt-math.org/zmath/en/advanced/?q=an:0844.53052&format=complete"} ZMATH entry
          %p
            For more on this see the references at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+AQFT"} perturbative AQFT
          %p See also
          %ul
            %li
              Springer online
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/eom"} eom
              %em
                %a{:href => "http://eom.springer.de/g/g043810.htm"} generalized function
          %p
            References on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Colombeau+algebra"} Colombeau algebra
            include
          %ul
            %li
              J. F. Colombeau,
              = succeed "," do
                %em New generalized functions and multiplications of distributions
              = succeed "," do
                %em Elementary introduction in new generalized functions
            %li
              N. Djapić, S. Pilipović,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Microlocal analysis of Colombeau’s generalized functions on a manifold
            %li
              Stevan Pilipović, Milica Žigić,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Suppleness of the sheaf of algebras of generalized functions on manifolds
              = succeed ":2" do
                %strong 379
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1101.4552"} arxiv/1101.4552
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=2784335"} MR2784335
              %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.jmaa.2010.12.060"} doi
          %h3#InTermsOfSmoothToposes In terms of smooth toposes
          %p
            Discussion of distributions in terms morphisms out of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+homs"} internal homs
            in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+topos"} smooth topos
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributions+are+the+smooth+linear+functionals"}> distributions are the smooth linear functionals
            ) is in
          %ul
            %li#MoerdijkReyes91
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ieke+Moerdijk"}> Ieke Moerdijk
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gonzalo+Reyes"} Gonzalo Reyes
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Models+for+Smooth+Infinitesimal+Analysis"} Models for Smooth Infinitesimal Analysis
          %p
            and for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cahiers+topos"} Cahiers topos
            in
          %ul
            %li#KockReyes03
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Anders+Kock"}> Anders Kock
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gonzalo+Reyes"} Gonzalo Reyes
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Some calculus with extensive quantities: wave equation
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/volumes/11/14/11-14abs.html"} TAC
            %li#KockReyes04
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Anders+Kock"}> Anders Kock
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gonzalo+Reyes"} Gonzalo Reyes
                %em Categorical distribution theory; heat equation
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0407242"}> arXiv:math/0407242
                )
            %li#Kock11
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Anders+Kock"}> Anders Kock
                ,
                %em Commutative monads as a theory of distributions
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1108.5952"}> arxiv/1108.5952
                )
          %p using results of
          %ul
            %li#FroelicherKriegl88
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alfred+Fr%C3%B6licher"}> Alfred Frölicher
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andreas+Kriegl"} Andreas Kriegl
              = succeed "," do
                %em Linear spaces and differentiation theory
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.fuw.edu.pl/~kostecki/scans/froelicherkriegl1988.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            and following the general conception of “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intensive+and+extensive"}> intensive and extensive
            ” in
          %ul
            %li#Lawvere86
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"}> William Lawvere
              , Introduction to
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Categories+in+Continuum+Physics"}> Categories in Continuum Physics
                , Lectures given at a Workshop held at SUNY, Buffalo 1982. Lecture Notes in Mathematics 1174. 1986
          %p
            Similar
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf+theory"} sheaf theoretic
            discussion of distributions as morphisms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+spaces"} smooth spaces
            is in
          %ul
            %li#Paugam12
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fr%C3%A9d%C3%A9ric+Paugam"}> Frédéric Paugam
              , section 3.2 of
              = succeed "," do
                %em Towards the mathematics of quantum field theory
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://webusers.imj-prg.fr/~frederic.paugam/documents/enseignement/master-mathematical-physics.pdf"} pdf
          .property
            category:
            %a.category_link{:href => "/nlab/all_pages/analysis"} analysis
